{"context":"In v1.x, framework executables are discovered from a closed set, and the adapter lifecycle/compatibility machinery is dead code (D9). In v2, delivery lifecycle standards are independent artifacts in independent repositories with their own versioning and release cadence (ADR-021, A5–A6). Distribution therefore needs a discovery, installation, and validation mechanism that v1.x does not have. Source: ADR-023-delivery-lifecycle-standard-registry.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"Adopt the delivery lifecycle standard registry concept defined in ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §3 A4 and §8 (ADR-023 row):\n\n- **Replaces closed-set adapter discovery.** The registry replaces closed-set discovery as the distribution path for delivery lifecycle standards (A4).\n- **Validates at adoption.** Adoption-time validation checks the declared contract version, the capability declaration (ADR-021), and trust attestation and integrity (ADR-022). The registry validates; the Anvil Runtime (Core) additionally verifies at invocation — compatibility negotiation at adoption is registry validation plus runtime verification (ADR-021 §3.4).\n- **Registry concept.** The registry comprises metadata format, discovery, installation/update semantics, and an offline/bundled install path.\n- **Installation s","consequences":"**Positive:** an open, validated distribution path for standards; compatibility errors surface at install, not in production; trust is validated from the first registry release (ADR-022); D9 is re-formed, not carried.\n\n**Negative:** a new registry client and metadata schema must be built (EPIC-014); migration from closed-set discovery requires the dual-run window (§12.4); metadata must be designed runtime-agnostically, which constrains the schema; community distribution benefits remain gated (Phase 7).","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Keep closed-set discovery.** Rejected: it cannot distribute standards from independent repositories (A6), and D9 r